Host-side unit tests
====================

Run them with:

    pio test -e native

They cover the modules that have no hardware or ESP-IDF dependency, so they
build with the host's own C compiler (gcc or clang must be on PATH):

    test_hvac_logic/    every rule in docs/CONTROL_SPEC.md and the hard safety
                        rules: timers, hysteresis, O changes, aux staging,
                        e-heat, faults, purge, auto changeover, setpoints, and
                        a long mixed run checked against the invariants
    test_relays_guard/  the independent relay interlock: bad requests, state
                        corruption, an all-off request dropping O and
                        re-arming, and 4M randomised steps checked from the
                        outputs alone
    test_safety_chain/  hvac_logic -> relays_guard as wired in the firmware,
                        guard on a skewed clock, random resets, fault bursts
                        (1-400 s) and mode changes; every hard rule checked
                        on the pins' clock, including all outputs off on
                        every fault step and minimum run waived only for a
                        fault, Off or e-heat (12 seeds x ~4.6 simulated days)
    test_sensor_logic/  SHT40 CRC and conversion, the 0-120 F plausibility
                        window, 2 min fault and 1 min recovery, self-heat lag
    test_boot_guard/    the crash-loop breaker's count across resets
    test_sensor_sim/    the bench simulated room, and a 2 h closed loop with
                        hvac_logic in Auto that must both heat and cool
    test_button_fsm/    D-pad debounce, press and auto-repeat
    test_settings/      defaults, range checks, setpoint pair reset
    test_pages/         UI navigation and setpoint keys; display formatting
    test_bridge_map/    Matter <-> thermostat values: units, modes, fan, state
    test_status_led/    bench LED blink patterns (bench builds only)
    test_display_shift/ OLED burn-in creep: offsets over time, frame shifting

Before every test run and firmware build, tools/check_safety_sources.py checks that only
relays.c can reach the relay pins (see the script for the rules).

Each test compiles the module under test directly into its own translation
unit (`#include "../../components/<module>/<file>.c"`). That keeps the
native build free of ESP-IDF and of any build-system plumbing, at the cost of
one deliberate deviation from the coding standard (D4 in
docs/CODING_STANDARD.md). Production code never includes a .c file.

unity_config.h exists so PlatformIO does not generate its own Unity glue,
which would be compiled with the strict flags and fail them. The strict
flags are in build_src_flags, which reach the tests but not Unity itself.

Adding a test: create test/test_<name>/test_main.c with setUp(), tearDown()
and a main() that runs the cases, and add the module's include directory to
build_flags in [env:native] if it is not already there.
